window.ATLAS_DECKS["knowledge"] = {"id":"knowledge","title":"Knowledge and Scepticism","question":"What can we really know?","topic":"reality","topicName":"Knowledge","minutes":40,"basis":"Draws on themes from Jennifer Nagel, Knowledge: A Very Short Introduction, and Plato’s Republic and Theaetetus","cover":"library","images":{"clock":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"A wall clock · tawalker · CC BY 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/30139045@N00/9769933853","pos":"50% 40%"},"fog":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Trees and ruin in fog · cyberdee · CC BY-NC-SA 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/79555058@N08/23312808534","pos":"50% 40%"},"barn":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Barn below the Tetons · Unknown · CC0","file":"https://www.rawpixel.com/image/3296123/free-photo-image-moose-teton-range-park","pos":"50% 40%"},"library":{"src":"img/deck/knowledge/library.webp","credit":"Library stacks · rosefirerising · CC BY-NC-ND 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/69145729@N00/556806349","pos":"50% 40%"},"zebra":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"A zebra · James F Clay · CC BY-NC 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/7888746@N08/1379943696","pos":"50% 40%"},"dream":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Asleep on a sofa · bronx. · CC BY-ND 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/23782487@N08/8536748781","pos":"50% 40%"},"lab":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Scientist at a microscope · IITA Image Library · CC BY-NC 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/45796762@N03/4923066809","pos":"50% 40%"},"eye":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"A human eye · François @ Edito.qc.ca · CC BY-NC-ND 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/91569742@N00/134041488","pos":"50% 40%"},"mirage":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Mirage on a desert highway · Symic · CC BY 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/7394270676","pos":"50% 40%"},"butterfly":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Chinese painting with butterflies · themet · CC0","file":"https://www.rawpixel.com/image/8054225/cat-and-butterfly","pos":"50% 40%"}},"slides":[{"layout":"title","kicker":"PUBLIC LECTURE · EPISTEMOLOGY","title":"Knowledge and Scepticism","question":"What can we really know?","sub":"Belief, truth and justification; luck and illusion; and the sceptic who doubts it all","tags":["BELIEF","GETTIER","DOUBT","SENSES","SCIENCE"],"img":"library","notes":"You know that two plus two is four, that you have hands, that the sun will rise tomorrow, that Orion is up there tonight. Or do you? Epistemology, from the Greek episteme, knowledge, asks what knowledge is, how we get it, and whether we have as much as we think.\nTonight we’ll try to define knowledge, watch the definition break, meet the sceptics of Greece, India and China, and ask how science manages to know anything at all.\nNext: three different kinds of knowing."},{"layout":"columns","section":"What we mean","title":"‘Know’ covers three different things","sub":"Epistemology is mostly about the first","cols":[{"label":"Propositional","title":"Knowing that","text":"Knowing that Paris is in France, or that water is H₂O. The main topic of epistemology.","img":"library"},{"label":"Practical","title":"Knowing how","text":"Knowing how to ride a bike or speak a language. You can do it without being able to say it (Ryle).","img":"lab"},{"label":"Acquaintance","title":"Knowing who or what","text":"Knowing a person, a city, the taste of mango: direct contact, not description (Russell).","img":"eye"}],"bottom":"Knowledge that is what can be true or false, so it is where the puzzles live.","notes":"English uses one word for several things. Knowing that something is true is propositional knowledge. Knowing how to ride a bicycle is a skill; Gilbert Ryle argued it can’t be reduced to knowing facts. And knowing a person or a place is acquaintance, which Bertrand Russell contrasted with knowing things only by description.\nMost of tonight is about knowing that, because it is the kind that can be true or false, justified or lucky.\nNext: Plato’s picture of the levels of knowledge."},{"layout":"ladder","section":"Plato","title":"Plato’s divided line: from shadows to the Forms","sub":"Republic, Book VI: four levels of mind, from least to most clear","rungs":[{"title":"Understanding (noesis)","text":"Grasping the Forms themselves, up to the Form of the Good, through pure reason.","tag":"KNOWLEDGE"},{"title":"Thought (dianoia)","text":"Mathematical reasoning: using diagrams and assumptions to reach necessary truths.","tag":"KNOWLEDGE"},{"title":"Belief (pistis)","text":"Opinions about the visible things around us: trees, animals, objects.","tag":"OPINION"},{"title":"Imagining (eikasia)","text":"Taking shadows and reflections for reality, like the prisoners in the cave.","tag":"OPINION"}],"bottom":"For Plato, knowledge is of what is unchanging; mere opinion is of what changes.","link":"#thinker-plato","linkLabel":"Meet Plato","notes":"In the Republic, Plato divides a line into four parts. At the bottom, imagining: taking shadows and reflections for real things. Above it, belief about ordinary objects. Then thought: the mathematician’s reasoning, which still leans on diagrams. At the top, understanding: grasping the Forms, the perfect unchanging patterns behind things, by reason alone.\nThe divided line goes with the allegory of the cave. The prisoners who see only shadows are at the bottom; the freed prisoner who sees the sun climbs to the top.\nNext: Plato’s other legacy, a definition of knowledge."},{"layout":"split","section":"The definition","title":"Knowledge as justified true belief, and how Gettier broke it","sub":"Plato’s Theaetetus (c. 369 BCE) to Edmund Gettier (1963)","challenge":{"title":"The classic analysis","text":"You know something if and only if you believe it, it is true, and you have good reason to believe it."},"answer":{"title":"The stopped clock","text":"You glance at a clock that reads two o’clock and believe it is two. It is two, but the clock stopped yesterday. Justified, true, believed, yet not knowledge."},"open":{"title":"The lesson","text":"Justification plus truth can still be luck. Something more is needed, and sixty years later philosophers still disagree about what."},"img":"clock","bottom":"A three-page paper (Gettier, 1963) overturned a definition that had lasted two thousand years.","notes":"In Plato’s Theaetetus, Socrates suggests knowledge is true belief with an account. Twentieth-century philosophers tidied this into justified true belief.\nIn 1963 Edmund Gettier published three pages showing that you can have a justified true belief by luck. Russell had already given an example: you look at a clock that has stopped, it happens to show the right time, and you believe it. Your belief is justified (clocks are usually reliable) and true, but you don’t know the time.\nNext: two more famous cases."},{"layout":"compare","section":"The definition","title":"Luck and reliability: two famous thought experiments","sub":"What does a knower need beyond true belief?","a":{"name":"Fake barn county","label":"Goldman · Ginet · 1976","lead":"Driving through the countryside, you see a barn and believe ‘that’s a barn’. It is. But unknown to you, the area is full of fake barn facades.","points":["Your belief is true and well formed","You could easily have been fooled"],"focus":"Do you know it’s a barn?","img":"barn"},"b":{"name":"The painted mule","label":"Dretske · 1970","lead":"At the zoo you see a zebra and know it’s a zebra. Do you know it isn’t a mule cleverly painted with stripes?","points":["You can’t rule that out by looking","Yet you seem to know it’s a zebra"],"focus":"Must you rule out every alternative?","img":"zebra"},"bottom":"Many epistemologists now think knowledge needs a belief that couldn’t easily have been wrong.","notes":"Alvin Goldman’s fake barn case: in a county full of facades, you happen to look at the one real barn. Most people feel you don’t know it’s a barn, because you could so easily have been fooled.\nFred Dretske’s zebra case points the other way. You know it’s a zebra, but you can’t rule out a painted mule. Must knowledge rule out every alternative, or only the relevant ones?\nNext: four answers to Gettier."},{"layout":"columns","section":"The definition","title":"Four ways to repair the definition of knowledge","sub":"Responses to Gettier","cols":[{"label":"No false steps","title":"No false lemmas","text":"Knowledge can’t rest on a false belief along the way.","img":"fog"},{"label":"Reliabilism","title":"A reliable process","text":"Knowledge is true belief produced by a reliable process, like good eyesight (Goldman).","img":"th:goldman"},{"label":"Virtue","title":"Credit to the knower","text":"Knowledge is true belief reached because of intellectual virtue, not luck (Zagzebski, Sosa).","img":"th:zagzebski"},{"label":"Knowledge first","title":"Don’t define it","text":"Knowledge is basic; belief and evidence are defined from it (Williamson).","img":"th:williamson"}],"bottom":"Externalists say you needn’t be able to tell that your belief is reliable; internalists say you must.","notes":"Some said knowledge just can’t rest on a false step. Reliabilists such as Alvin Goldman said the belief must come from a reliable process. Virtue epistemologists such as Linda Zagzebski and Ernest Sosa say the knower must get it right because of their abilities, as an archer hits the target by skill rather than wind. Timothy Williamson says we should stop trying: knowledge is the basic notion.\nNext: the sceptic, who doubts we know anything."},{"layout":"gallery","section":"Scepticism","title":"Sceptics in many traditions have asked whether we know anything at all","sub":"Dreams, illusions, and the endless regress of reasons","items":[{"img":"th:pyrrho","caption":"Pyrrho","text":"Greece, c. 300 BCE: suspend judgment and find peace"},{"img":"th:sextus","caption":"Sextus Empiricus","text":"Every reason needs a reason: regress, circle or assumption"},{"img":"butterfly","caption":"Zhuangzi’s butterfly","text":"Am I a man who dreamt of being a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming I am a man?"},{"img":"th:nagarjuna","caption":"Nagarjuna","text":"India, c. 200 CE: every means of knowing needs another to prove it"},{"img":"dream","caption":"Descartes’ dream","text":"No sure sign tells waking from dreaming"},{"img":"mirage","caption":"The mirage","text":"Water on the road: Nyaya and Buddhist debates on illusion"}],"bottom":"Scepticism isn’t a school but a challenge every theory of knowledge must answer.","link":"#agora-skepticism","linkLabel":"Debate: can we know anything?","notes":"Pyrrho and Sextus Empiricus in Greece taught that for every argument there is an equal argument against, so we should suspend judgment, and in doing so find tranquillity. Sextus’s trilemma: every justification either goes on for ever, goes round in a circle, or stops at an assumption.\nZhuangzi in China dreamt he was a butterfly and woke unsure which he was. Nagarjuna in India argued that every means of knowledge needs another to establish it. Indian philosophers argued for centuries about the mirage: when you see water on a hot road, what exactly are you seeing?\nNext: Descartes’ method of doubt."},{"layout":"steps","section":"Scepticism","title":"Descartes doubted everything he could, to find what could not be doubted","sub":"Meditations on First Philosophy, 1641","steps":[{"title":"The senses","text":"They sometimes deceive me, like the mirage or the stick bent in water."},{"title":"The dream","text":"I can’t be sure I’m not dreaming right now."},{"title":"The demon","text":"An evil genius might deceive me even about mathematics."},{"title":"The cogito","text":"But I can’t doubt that I am doubting. I think, therefore I am."}],"bottom":"Today’s version is the brain in a vat: can you prove you’re not in a perfect simulation?","notes":"Descartes wanted foundations as firm as geometry. So he doubted everything he could. The senses sometimes deceive. Even if they don’t, he might be dreaming. Even mathematics might be the work of an all-powerful deceiving demon. What survives? The fact that he is thinking. ‘Cogito, ergo sum’: I think, therefore I am.\nThe modern version is the brain in a vat, or the simulation hypothesis.\nNext: where knowledge comes from."},{"layout":"compare","section":"Sources","title":"Does knowledge come from reason or from experience?","sub":"The great debate of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries","a":{"name":"Rationalism","label":"Descartes · Spinoza · Leibniz","lead":"Reason alone can reach truths about the world.","points":["Some ideas are innate, like God or infinity","Mathematics is the model of knowledge"],"focus":"Certainty through reason","img":"th:descartes"},"b":{"name":"Empiricism","label":"Locke · Berkeley · Hume","lead":"The mind begins as a blank slate; all ideas come from experience.","points":["No innate ideas","Knowledge of the world is never certain"],"focus":"Knowledge through the senses","img":"th:locke"},"bottom":"Kant’s synthesis: experience provides the content, but the mind supplies the forms (space, time, cause).","link":"#thinker-kant","linkLabel":"Meet Kant","notes":"Rationalists such as Descartes and Leibniz thought reason could discover truths about the world, and that some ideas are innate. Empiricists such as Locke, Berkeley and Hume said the mind starts blank and everything comes through the senses.\nKant tried to combine them: ‘thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind’. Experience gives the material, but the mind shapes it.\nNext: the Indian theory of the means of knowledge."},{"layout":"table","section":"Sources","title":"Indian philosophy asked: how many ‘means of knowledge’ are there?","sub":"Pramana theory: schools disagreed about the list","head":["School","Accepted means of knowledge","Why"],"rows":[["Charvaka","Perception only","Inference goes beyond what we see"],["Buddhist (Dignaga, Dharmakirti)","Perception, inference","Testimony is a kind of inference"],["Nyaya (Gautama)","Perception, inference, comparison, testimony","Reliable words are a source on their own"],["Mimamsa / Vedanta","Add postulation and non-perception","To know absences and hidden causes"]],"bottom":"Is testimony from others a source of knowledge in its own right? It’s still debated today.","notes":"Indian philosophers built a careful theory of pramanas, means of valid knowledge. The materialist Charvaka school accepted only perception. Buddhists such as Dignaga and Dharmakirti accepted perception and inference. The Nyaya school added comparison and testimony, the word of a reliable person. Others added more.\nThe question of testimony is very modern: almost everything you know, you learned from someone else. Is that knowledge in its own right?\nNext: how science knows."},{"layout":"columns","section":"Science","title":"How can science know anything, if it always goes beyond the evidence?","sub":"Induction and its critics","cols":[{"label":"The problem","title":"Hume’s induction","text":"The sun has always risen. But no logic proves the future will resemble the past.","img":"th:hume"},{"label":"A solution","title":"Popper’s falsification","text":"Science doesn’t prove theories; it tests them and throws out the ones that fail.","img":"th:popper"},{"label":"A twist","title":"Kuhn’s paradigms","text":"Science works inside paradigms that change in revolutions, not by pure logic.","img":"th:kuhn"}],"bottom":"Science is our most successful way of knowing, yet philosophers still argue over why it works.","notes":"Hume noticed that all reasoning about the future assumes the future will resemble the past, and that assumption can’t be proved without circularity. Karl Popper responded that science never proves theories: it makes bold conjectures and tries to refute them. Thomas Kuhn showed that real science works within paradigms which occasionally collapse in revolutions.\nNext: a toolkit."},{"layout":"steps","section":"Take it with you","title":"Four questions to ask of any claim to know","sub":"A toolkit","steps":[{"title":"Is it true?","text":"Knowledge requires truth, not just confidence."},{"title":"What’s the source?","text":"Perception, reasoning, testimony or memory?"},{"title":"Could it be luck?","text":"Would you still believe it if it were false?"},{"title":"How sure must we be?","text":"Does it need certainty, or just good evidence?"}],"bottom":"Most of everyday knowledge is fallible, and that is fine.","notes":"Four questions. Is it true? Where does it come from? Could you have got it right by luck? And what standard are we using: certainty, or good evidence? Most philosophers now accept fallibilism: we can know things without being absolutely certain.\nNext: what the field leaves us with."},{"layout":"closing","title":"Epistemology doesn’t end doubt; it teaches us how to handle it","sub":"Better questions","questions":["Is knowledge just true belief that isn’t lucky?","Can we answer the sceptic, or only ignore them?","How much of what you know rests on trusting others?"],"line":"The wise person knows what they don’t know.","notes":"Socrates said his only wisdom was knowing that he didn’t know. Epistemology doesn’t abolish doubt; it teaches us to proportion our confidence."},{"layout":"discussion","title":"Discussion: bring the theory back to real life","sub":"Three questions for debate","questions":["How do you know the news you read is true?","Do you know that you’re not dreaming right now?","When should you trust an expert over your own judgment?"],"line":"Name your source of knowledge first.","link":"#compass-reality","linkLabel":"Take the Compass: Reality","notes":"Three questions. For each, say first what source of knowledge you are relying on."}]};
